types.doc:
A stream message read as the typed event it is: the order, position, terminal, candle, Risk Management and copy events. [Of](#stream.MfxEvent.Of) reads one message, or answers null for any other type.

```csharp
await foreach (var e in stream)
{
    if (e is StreamMessage m && MfxEvent.Of(m) is OrderClosed { Reason: "filled" } closed)
        Console.WriteLine($"order {closed.Ticket} became position {closed.PositionTicket}");
}
```

Ids are `Guid`s, tickets and magic numbers `Int64`s, prices, volumes and money `Decimal`s, and times `DateTimeOffset`s. The platform's own words (a side, an order type, a reason, a state, a timeframe) stay text: they are not a closed list, and a word added later reads as it was written instead of failing the read. The message keeps its data whatever this returns, so nothing is lost by a type this version does not know.

Follows one terminal's account live on `account:{terminal_id}`, snapshot then marks, by the stream's ordering rules. It holds no socket: the caller drives the stream and hands it every event.

```csharp
var live = new LiveAccount(terminalId);
await foreach (var e in stream)
{
    if (live.Apply(e) is { } view) Show(view);
    else if (live.Behind) await stream.ResumeAsync(Channel.Account(terminalId));
}
```

It never guesses: a message it cannot apply is dropped whole and [Behind](#stream.LiveAccount.Behind) goes true; it does not half-apply, and it does not patch a position it has never seen. Only a snapshot clears [Behind](#stream.LiveAccount.Behind); one arrives within 60 s on its own, or at once on a resume. A mark is positional, `[ticket, price_current, profit, swap]`, and is read by position.
